% I strongly disagree.  If you were in a room and you saw a hole in the floor
% at the other end and starting walking towards it and the lights went out,
% would you remember that there was a hole or would you assume that the room
% had suddenly changed?  ("Trust your memory, not your eyes.  Your eyes can
% lie" - Chiun in Remo Williams)  I agree that it complicates things if you
% expect the program to figure out if you would remember something or not, so
% give the person the choice.

An alternative solution is to make the above depend on the character's
intelligence.  Thus a blinded wizard would realize that he was blinded,
recall that he'd seen a trap/hole/etc. and stop.  The poor caveman on
the other hand would generally freak out on being blinded, have no idea
what was going on, and continue in a dazed fashion.

A general extension of this notion might to be add a new attribute to the
character.  Memory.  Be able to recall certain parts of the dungeon, 
recall what he read on fortune cookies, etc.  

What do you think?

